What is regicide and what are its historical and cultural connotations?
Regicide refers to the deliberate killing of a monarch or the person responsible for the killing of a person of royalty. Historically, it has been associated with the judicial execution of a king after a trial, as seen in the case of Charles I of England. More broadly, regicide can also refer to the killing of an emperor or any other reigning sovereign.

Regicide has significant cultural and historical connotations, particularly in relation to the concept of the divine right of kings. This concept held that monarchs were divinely appointed to rule, making an attack on a king by one of his own subjects a grave offense. Regicide has been used as a metaphor for the public's treatment of celebrities, where a star's popularity fades and they are forgotten.

In some ancient cultures, regicide was a ritualistic practice, where a king was killed at the end of their 7-year rule. This practice has been seen as a symbol of the cycle of life and death, where an old identity must be put to an end to make way for a new one.

Regicide has also been associated with significant events in history, such as the Lisbon Regicide, where King Carlos I of Portugal and his heir-apparent were assassinated by Republican sympathizers. The concept of regicide continues to be relevant in modern times, with its historical and cultural connotations serving as a reminder of the complexities of power, authority, and mortality.
