What is the role and function of the lower house in the British parliament?
In the British parliament, the lower house, also known as the House of Commons, is the de facto legislative body. Its primary role is to make laws, and it holds much more legislative power than the upper house, the House of Lords. The House of Commons is composed of elected members, known as Members of Parliament (MPs), who represent constituencies in the UK. They are responsible for debating and voting on bills, which are then sent to the upper house for review and approval. The House of Commons also has the power to hold the government accountable through various means, such as questioning ministers and conducting investigations.
