Based on the provided context, Christopher Jones' career as a ship captain can be identified as follows:

* He served his apprenticeship at the side of his father and grandfather, who were both Harwich skippers, in the North Sea.
* At the age of 18, he inherited his first part-share of a ship.
* He was a master and part-owner of the "Mayflower" in 1609, when it was chartered for a voyage from London to Drontheim (Trondheim) in Norway, and back to London.
* He was the Captain of the 1620 voyage of the Pilgrim ship "Mayflower".
* He was involved in litigation in 1612 due to the "Mayflower" losing an anchor and making a short delivery of its cargo of herrings on its return from Norway.

Overall, Christopher Jones' career as a ship captain spanned several decades, from his apprenticeship in the North Sea to his command of the "Mayflower" on its historic voyage to America in 1620.
