What is the historical or literary context of the Robin Hood stories, specifically regarding the character of Little John?
The historical or literary context of the Robin Hood stories, specifically regarding the character of Little John, is rooted in medieval English folklore and literature. Little John is depicted as Robin Hood's chief lieutenant and second-in-command of the Merry Men in the earliest recorded Robin Hood ballads and stories, dating back to the 15th century. He appears in various tales, including "A Gest of Robyn Hode" and "Robin Hood's Death", showcasing his intelligence and capabilities. Little John's character has evolved over time, with some portrayals depicting him as less cunning and others highlighting his leadership skills. He has been featured in numerous adaptations, including films, plays, and ballads, solidifying his place in the Robin Hood canon.
