The historical context surrounding the establishment of the law enforcement agency in 1908 is that it was created as the Bureau of Investigation (BOI) within the Department of Justice, established by Attorney General Charles Joseph Bonaparte, who was known as "Charlie, the Crook Chaser," and was active in breaking up trusts and monopolies, including the tobacco monopoly. The agency was initially staffed with 34 people, including veterans, and was later renamed the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) in 1935.
